dar, reconstructed from the 1.67 patch
=====================================

There is no source for dar, so cmd/dar is the disassembly, and this README
records what the binary is before it is read back into C.  dar.dist is a
copy of /bin/dar off the Micronix 1.67 patch (disks/Late/mw1.67_patch/bin/dar);
dar.dis is disas's output from it, regenerated with "make disas".

dar is the DOS-diskette file-exchange utility: it reads an IBM/DOS-format
floppy in a Micronix drive and copies files between the two filesystems.
There is no dar.1 in the patch, so the interface below is read out of the
binary's own strings and the disassembly, not a man page.

    usage: dar device {cdprtx} files ...

The options are c (copy FROM the DOS diskette), p (copy TO the DOS
diskette), and d, r, t, x, of which "At most one of d, r, t, x may be
specified" - the single-file operations, still to be read apart.  With no
operation option dar runs its menu, which its strings spell out:

    A) Copy from the DOS diskette
    B) Copy to the DOS diskette
    C) Display file on the DOS diskette
    D) Quit

and prompts "Enter disk name:", "Insert diskette and close the drive
door", "File to copy (or RETURN for all files):", "File to delete:" and
"Selection:".  It reads /etc/mtab for the mounted device ("/dev/" is its
prefix) and reports space as "Device size", "Diskette", "% full," and
"K free space left", and file dates with "%s %2d %2d:%02d ".

Its diagnostics: "Not a DOS diskette!", ": Unknown format", ": Not block
special", ": Can't allocate directory entry", "Not enough space left on
volume" / ": Volume full", ": duplicate file name", ": Is a directory"
and ": No such file or directory".

Layout: Whitesmith's C.  text 0x84fb bytes at 0x0100, data 0x1d10 at
0x85fb, no bss and no symbol table.  The DOS directory entry, the FAT and
the sector buffer are the globals to find in the data segment; the menu,
the option scan, and the three copy/display/delete operations are the
user text.  This is the heavy lift of the patch batch - the largest of
the seven - so the reconstruction is expected to come in pieces.

